# freetsa.org's Root CA certificate — the trust anchor for offline
# verification of RFC 3161 anchor receipts (backend/api/src/audit/anchor.rs,
# scripts/bundle/verify_bundle.py step 7c).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY IT MUST BE A COMMITTED, PINNED COPY
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An RFC 3161 `TimeStampToken` embeds the certificate that signed it AND
# (for freetsa.org, confirmed empirically while writing this module) the
# certificate of the CA that issued that signing certificate. Verifying a
# signature against certificates carried INSIDE the thing being verified
# proves nothing on its own — anyone can construct a self-signed CA, issue
# themselves a "TSA" certificate under it, and forge a token that is
# internally consistent by that measure alone. What makes an anchor a real
# witness is comparing the embedded CA certificate against a copy this
# deployment obtained and pinned BEFORE the token existed, independently of
# whatever the token itself claims. This file is that independent copy.
#
# It ships inside every evidence bundle (scripts/bundle/build_bundle.py,
# written as `tsa_ca_chain.pem`) rather than being fetched by the verifier,
# for the same reason `jwks_snapshot.json` is captured rather than fetched
# live: `verify_bundle.py` runs air-gapped, and a check that phones home for
# its own trust anchor is not an offline check.
#
# PROVENANCE
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Downloaded from https://freetsa.org/files/cacert.pem on 2026-08-20 while
# this module was being written, over TLS, and cross-checked against the CA
# certificate embedded in a real granted TimeStampToken obtained from
# https://freetsa.org/tsr the same day (`openssl ts -reply -in response.tsr
# -text` and `openssl pkcs7 -inform DER -in token.der -print_certs` both
# show this same Root CA, subject == issuer, self-signed, matching
# byte-for-byte). `openssl ts -verify -CAfile <this file> -untrusted
# <the TSA signing cert> ...` against that real token: `Verification: OK`.
#
# WHAT PINNING THIS DOES AND DOES NOT BUY
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It removes "the token vouches for itself" as a way to forge an anchor.
# It does NOT remove freetsa.org as a single point of trust: this is a
# community-run, unaudited CA (see audit/anchor.rs's header), and anyone who
# could compel or compromise ITS private key could backdate an anchor this
# check would still accept. Swap `MEMTARA_ANCHOR_TSA_URL` and this pinned
# file together before that risk matters for a real deployment — a
# commercially audited TSA's root certificate is the same kind of file, just
# a different, more accountable, issuer.

